Practice Problems — Topic 2
Q1. Series-parallel array design:
Available panel: 330W, V_mp=32.5V, I_mp=10.15A, V_oc=39.8V, I_sc=10.72A.
Inverter specifications: MPPT range 200-800V, max input current 18A, max input power 10kW.
(a) Maximum panels in series before exceeding 800V MPPT limit
(b) Check V_oc_array at T=10°C (use β_Voc=−0.34%/°C) for your chosen N_s. Does it exceed 800V?
(c) For a 6.6kW system: how many panels total? What is the N_s × N_p configuration that satisfies both the voltage and current limits?
(d) Total array P_max, V_array_mpp, I_array_mpp for your chosen configuration
(e) Energy generated per day at 5.5 peak sun hours with 80% system efficiency
Q2. P-V curve analysis:
A 72-cell panel: I_sc=10.2A, V_oc=45.6V, I_mp=9.5A, V_mp=37.8V.
(a) P_max and fill factor
(b) Power at V = 0.8 × V_mp (is it still close to P_max?)
(c) Power at V = 1.1 × V_mp
(d) Power at V = 0.5 × V_mp
(e) Sketch (describe in words) the shape of the P-V curve, identifying where P=0, where P=P_max, and how quickly power falls on each side of the peak
Q3. Partial shading analysis:
A string of 5 panels in series (each 300W, V_mp=30V, I_mp=10A). Each panel has one bypass diode.
(a) Normal (no shading): V_string, I_string, P_string
(b) One panel completely shaded (bypass diode activates): V_string, I_string, P_string
(c) Two adjacent panels shaded to 30% irradiance (bypass diodes NOT activated, assuming mismatch current forces them to operate at reduced current): I_string ≈ 0.3 × 10 = 3A, V_string ≈ 5 × 30 = 150V (approximately, since V_mp barely changes). P_string.
(d) Compare (b) and (c): which shading scenario is worse for power output?
(e) Describe two methods to mitigate partial shading losses in a rooftop PV system. (Hint: consider panel orientation and power electronics solutions)
Q4. Array configuration for Nigerian off-grid system:
A rural health clinic in Kano needs a solar system to power: refrigerator (150W continuous), LED lighting (300W for 6 hours/night), medical equipment (500W for 4 hours/day), phone charging (100W for 8 hours/day). Battery bank: 48V nominal, capacity sized for 2 days autonomy. Available panel: 250W, V_mp=30.5V, I_mp=8.2A.
(a) Total daily energy demand (Wh/day)
(b) With 6 peak sun hours and 75% system efficiency: required solar array capacity (Wp)
(c) Number of 250W panels required
(d) For a 48V battery system: charge controller input should accept array voltage. Design the array configuration (N_s × N_p) for a MPPT charge controller with input range 60-150V
(e) Total array cost estimate if panels cost ₦65,000 each, plus 30% for mounting, wiring, and installation
